# Project One Get-Item Pseudocode
#
# Graded checkpoint: Module Five Project One
# Later use: get-item and inventory design for M7 Project Two
#
# Review the course-provided get-item flowchart before completing this file.
# Write pseudocode, not Python code. Use clear indentation and course-appropriate
# keywords such as INPUT, OUTPUT, IF, ELSE, SET, ADD, or similar terms.

START get_item

    # TODO 1: # The item process needs:
    # - current_room: the room the player is currently in
    # - inventory: the list of items the player has collected

    # Consider the current room, item in that room, and player's inventory.

    # TODO 2: BEGIN get_item(current_room, inventory)

    IF current_room contains an item THEN
        item ← current_room.item
        ADD item to inventory
        REMOVE item from current_room
        DISPLAY "You picked up the " + item
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "There is no item to pick up here."
    END IF

END


    # TODO 3: # Item collection supports the game by allowing the player to gather the
    # required objects needed to win. Without item pickup, the player could not
    # complete objectives or progress toward the final challenge.

    

    

    # TODO 4: # When the item request is valid, the player successfully picks up the item in
    # the current room. The item is added to the player's inventory, and the item is
    # removed from the room so it cannot be collected again. The player receives
    # output confirming the item was picked up.

    

    # TODO 5: # When the item request is invalid, it means the current room does not contain
    # an item for the player to pick up. In this case, the inventory does not change
    # and no item is added. The player receives output informing them that there is
    # no item available in the room.

    

    # TODO 6: # After the item request is handled, the process returns control to the main
    # gameplay loop. The game continues by displaying the updated room information,
    # checking the player's inventory, and waiting for the next command. This allows
    # the player to keep exploring, moving, or collecting items as part of the
    # ongoing game flow.

    

END get_item


